  "id": "defn_intervals",
  "level": "2",
  "url": "sec_functions.html#defn_intervals",
  "type": "Definition",
  "number": "6.1.6",
  "title": "Intervals.",
  "body": " Intervals       An interval is a set of all real numbers between two real numbers known as bounds . For example, the set of all real numbers such that is an interval bounded below by 11 and bounded above by 12. The integer 11 is an element of the interval, but the integer 12 is not an element of the interval.    An unbounded interval (or ray ) is a set of all real numbers greater than a real number or a set of all real numbers less than a real number. For example, the set of all real numbers such that is an unbounded interval. The real number 4.32 is an element of that interval. The set of negative real numbers is the unbounded interval defined by . The real number 0 is not an element of that interval.    We can abbreviate an interval using interval notation where a square bracket indicates that the bound is included, whereas a round bracket indicates that the bound is excluded. When the interval is unbounded, we use to indicate that the interval has no upper bound and to indicate that the interval has no lower bound. For example, the interval described by the inequality is denoted and the positive real numbers is denoted . Occasionally we write the full set of real numbers as the interval . END shows examples of each type of interval notation.  Interval notation    Interval               Notation       \\vspace{.15in}   Unbounded Interval                 Notation               "
