  "title": "Sequence.",
  "body": " Sequence       A sequence is a function whose domain is a set of the form where is an integer. Note that when this set is and when this set is . For example, the function defined by is a sequence.    A sequence starts at  if its domain is . For example, the sequence from starts at 0 because the domain is . We primarily consider sequences that start at 0 or 1.    As a shorthand, we use sequence notation to write for . For example, the sequence from can be defined by . This notation specifies the domain and rule for the function but not the codomain and so it should only be used when the codomain is clear from the context or stated separately.    A sequence can be described using a list of its terms: . For example, in the sequence from we calculate and so the sequence corresponds to the list beginning .    We can list the first few terms of a sequence using a table , as in .  Displaying a sequence in a table                               For example, we can list the first terms of for in .  The first few terms of the sequence defined by for                                   The expression is pronounced sub . The (input) value of is the index of the term and the (output) value of is the term of the sequence. For example, in the sequence from , the term is and its index is .    A sequence is explicitly-defined (or in closed form ) if there is a rule for that depends only on and constants. For example, the sequence from is explicitly-defined.      "
