  "body": " Proper bit strings       Prove that the bit string is proper.  To see why, we start with the initial condition B that tells us is proper. We can use R1 to show that is proper, and then apply R1 again to show that is proper. Next, we apply R2 to show that is proper, and then apply R2 again to show that is proper. Finally, we apply R1 again to show that is proper.  We can summarize this series of steps in the diagram:       Prove that the bit string is improper.  We start with B which tells us is proper. If we first apply R1 , we get that 0 is proper. To get two more 1 s we can apply R2 but that tells us is proper, which is not our bit string. On the other hand, if we first apply R2 , we get that is proper. To get one 0 , we can apply R1 but that tells us 110 is proper, which is also not our bit string.  Alternatively, we can construct a possibility tree listing all bit strings of length up to three that are proper as shown in .   All proper bit strings of length three or less            "
